# Base URL of the API, as seen from the browser. Must include the /v1 prefix.
# The backend defaults to PORT=3100 in its own .env; `next dev` uses 3000.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:3100/v1

# Optional. Base URL as seen from the Next *server* (server components and
# generateMetadata). Set this when the API is reachable at a different address
# from inside the container than from the browser, e.g. http://api:3100/v1.
# Falls back to NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL.
# API_URL_INTERNAL=http://api:3100/v1

# Optional. Canonical site origin, used to build absolute share links during
# server rendering where window.location is unavailable.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://forms.example.com


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Security headers  (see src/lib/csp.ts)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Strict, nonce-based Content-Security-Policy on the two PUBLIC routes only:
# /f/* (form runner) and /a/* (form-app). Everything else keeps the baseline
# policy, so the prerendered marketing and docs pages stay static.
#
#   unset / anything else  off      — no change. THIS IS THE DEFAULT.
#   report                           — sends Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only.
#                                      Nothing is blocked; violations are logged
#                                      to the browser console. Always run this
#                                      first.
#   enforce                          — sends Content-Security-Policy. A script
#                                      the nonce misses is a BLANK FORM for every
#                                      respondent, so only set this after
#                                      `report` gave a clean console on a real
#                                      published form, in a production build.
#
# This is a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable, so it is baked in at build time: changing it
# requires a rebuild, not just a restart.
#
# How to verify, in order:
#   1. NEXT_PUBLIC_STRICT_CSP_PUBLIC_ROUTES=report, then `npm run build && npm run start`
#      (nonces need a production build; `next dev` uses eval-based HMR).
#   2. curl -sI http://localhost:3000/f/<a-real-published-slug> | grep -i content-security
#      -> expect TWO headers: the baseline `Content-Security-Policy` and a
#         `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only` containing `'nonce-...'`
#         and `'strict-dynamic'`. If the report-only one is missing, the proxy
#         is not running on that path and nothing below will work.
#   3. Load that URL in a browser with DevTools open. The form must render and
#      be fillable. In the console you should see AT MOST ONE violation, for the
#      `next-themes` anti-flash inline script in the root layout, which cannot
#      be nonced without dropping the whole site to dynamic rendering.
#      ANY OTHER violation — especially one naming /_next/static/chunks/... —
#      means do not switch to `enforce`.
#   4. Also exercise: submitting the form, a multi-page form's next/back, file
#      upload, and /a/<slug>.
#   5. Only then rebuild with `enforce`. In that mode the proxy's header
#      REPLACES the baseline one on these two routes, so `curl -sI` should show
#      exactly one `Content-Security-Policy`, containing the nonce and
#      `frame-ancestors *`. If frame-ancestors is missing, embeds are broken.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STRICT_CSP_PUBLIC_ROUTES=report

# `upgrade-insecure-requests` in the CSP. OFF unless explicitly set to `true`.
#
# It makes the browser silently rewrite every http:// URL on the page to
# https://, including API calls. If the API is reachable over plain http in the
# real deployment topology — a sidecar, an internal load balancer, a bare-IP
# staging box, a LAN install on http://192.168.x.x — enabling this breaks
# EVERY API call, and only in the environment where it is enabled.
#
# Before setting it, confirm all three: the app is served over https; the API
# URL the browser uses is https with a valid (not self-signed) certificate; and
# every absolute URL that can appear in a page (author-supplied logo and cover
# images, S3 presigned upload endpoints) is reachable over https.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_UPGRADE_INSECURE_REQUESTS=true


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Error reporting  (see src/lib/sentry.ts)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Sentry DSN. Unset by default, and everything Sentry is inert without it: the
# SDK is not initialised, not bundled into the browser, and prints nothing.
#
# Note what this project scrubs before sending, and why (src/lib/sentry.ts):
# request bodies, cookies, query strings and any key named answers/values/
# responses/... are stripped, because on a form product those are respondent
# answers. `sendDefaultPii` is false and Session Replay is not enabled.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=https://<key>@<org>.ingest.sentry.io/<project>

# Optional. Performance tracing sample rate, 0..1. Defaults to 0 — transaction
# names here are URLs, and prefilled-form URLs carry answers in their query
# strings.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE=0.1

# Optional. Sentry environment tag. Defaults to NODE_ENV.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=production
